{"vid":"9FvQ9125Hjs","source":"9FvQ9125Hjs.en-en.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":14.6,"txt":"Hi, Tim here with another 925 English lesson."},{"t":20.1,"txt":"Today we’re going to learn how to explain a problem in English."},{"t":28.1,"txt":"Much of your work probably involves solving problems."},{"t":33.6,"txt":"And sometimes we can’t do it alone."},{"t":38.1,"txt":"But cooperating with someone to solve a problem means explaining it clearly."},{"t":46.1,"txt":"Before you explain the problem however, you’ll need to ask for help."},{"t":52.7,"txt":"Two really useful words in this situation are “would” and “could.”"},{"t":62.2,"txt":"These words help you make polite requests."},{"t":66.8,"txt":"For example, you might say “could you help me?” or “would you help me with this?”"},{"t":75.7,"txt":"And we can add to these expressions to get longer, even more polite expressions."},{"t":84,"txt":"Let’s practice some ways of asking for help using “would” and “could.”"},{"t":92.6,"txt":"Listen to each example, then repeat it for yourself."},{"t":96.7,"txt":"Ready?"},{"t":97.7,"txt":"Let’s get started."},{"t":101.9,"txt":"Do you think you could give us a hand with this?"},{"t":110.9,"txt":"Would you mind showing me how to do this?"},{"t":120.2,"txt":"Could you help me with this?"},{"t":126.5,"txt":"I would appreciate a bit of help."},{"t":135.5,"txt":"As you heard, we can build a polite sentence around “would” and “could, using questions"},{"t":144.4,"txt":"like “do you think you could” and “would you mind.”"},{"t":150.6,"txt":"This is much more polite than giving people direct orders, like “help me, please!”"},{"t":158.3,"txt":"Now, if someone agrees to help you, what do you say next?"},{"t":164.5,"txt":"Do you start describing the problem in great detail?"},{"t":169,"txt":"Or do you think it would be better to say generally what the problem is?"},{"t":175,"txt":"Well, at the start, it’s good to be more general."},{"t":180.1,"txt":"You can give a short overview of the problem to give the person a general idea of what"},{"t":186.7,"txt":"it’s about."},{"t":188.2,"txt":"Let’s try some examples of giving a general statement about a problem."},{"t":194.7,"txt":"Remember to repeat the examples after you hear them."},{"t":201,"txt":"It looks like there’s a safety issue at the factory."},{"t":207.8,"txt":"We’ve got some problems with the website."},{"t":219.5,"txt":"I can’t seem to get the copier to work."},{"t":229.2,"txt":" The security system isn’t working properly."},{"t":235.6,"txt":"Did you hear another word that has the same meaning as “problem?”"},{"t":244.4,"txt":"The word is “issue,” as in “there’s a safety issue.”"},{"t":251.2,"txt":"Or you could have a “technical issue,” or a “security issue,” or a “staffing"},{"t":260.5,"txt":"issue.”"},{"t":262.3,"txt":"Also notice that a lot of problems are about whether something “works.”"},{"t":268.4,"txt":"That just means whether it functions properly."},{"t":272.4,"txt":"Now, it’s not usually enough just to know that there’s a problem."},{"t":279.1,"txt":"If we hope to solve it, we usually need to know what happened to cause the problem or"},{"t":286.1,"txt":"make you think there was a problem."},{"t":288.6,"txt":"In this case, you need to tell a little story."},{"t":294.2,"txt":"And you should use the past tense to describe what happened."},{"t":299.9,"txt":"For example, you might say “I turned the computer on, but the screen was blank.”"},{"t":307.6,"txt":"Or you might say “X happened, when I did Y.”"},{"t":312.9,"txt":"And at this point, you should be specific and detailed about what happened."},{"t":319.5,"txt":"Let’s try some practice with describing what happened using this approach."},{"t":327,"txt":"Once again, repeat the examples after you hear them."},{"t":333.5,"txt":"The problem started when we made changes to the homepage."},{"t":343.7,"txt":"So I tried to save the document to the shared folder."},{"t":354.6,"txt":"The machine made a strange noise when I turned it on."},{"t":363.9,"txt":"I opened the program, but I couldn’t log in."},{"t":374.5,"txt":"Of course, explaining a problem clearly might mean making several sentences like this to"},{"t":381.9,"txt":"describe the situation."},{"t":384.9,"txt":"And with a clear understanding of the situation, hopefully the other person will be able to"},{"t":391.8,"txt":"help you solve it."},{"t":394.5,"txt":"Okay, so we’ve practiced lots of useful examples for talking about problems."},{"t":401.6,"txt":"But how do these expressions sound in a conversation?"},{"t":406.2,"txt":"Let’s listen to a short dialog between Kristi and John."},{"t":412.1,"txt":"John is trying to describe a problem with his website to Kristi."},{"t":417.3,"txt":"Let’s listen."},{"t":419.6,"txt":"Kristi: So what seems to be the problem?"},{"t":423.1,"txt":"John: It looks like there’s an issue with the website."},{"t":426.7,"txt":"Kristi: Oh no, what’s going on?"},{"t":430.1,"txt":"John: Well, it started when I updated the homepage."},{"t":437,"txt":"Simple enough, right?"},{"t":439.7,"txt":"When Kristi asks about the problem, John says there’s an “issue” with the website."},{"t":446.8,"txt":"And when Kristi asks for more detail, he explains that it started when he updated the homepage."},{"t":456.2,"txt":"Now it’s your turn to practice."},{"t":460.4,"txt":"We’ll repeat the dialog, but this time we’re going to beep out the second speaker’s words."},{"t":468.4,"txt":"You will have to say those parts yourself."},{"t":472.4,"txt":"Remember to say it looks like there’s an issue with the website, then say it started"},{"t":480.5,"txt":"when you updated the homepage."},{"t":483.6,"txt":"Kristi: So what seems to be the problem?"},{"t":486.9,"txt":"John: It looks like there’s an issue with the website."},{"t":493,"txt":"Kristi: Oh no, what’s going on?"},{"t":496.6,"txt":"John: Well, it started when I updated the homepage."},{"t":504.8,"txt":"All right, that’s all for this lesson!"},{"t":508.1,"txt":"We’ve learned some great ways to ask for help, make general statements about a problem,"},{"t":515,"txt":"and describe what happened."},{"t":517.3,"txt":"We’ll be back soon with some more useful English expressions."},{"t":523.1,"txt":"Until then, so long and happy learning!"}],"words":[{"t":14.58,"w":"Hi,"},{"t":14.58,"w":"Tim"},{"t":14.58,"w":"here"},{"t":14.58,"w":"with"},{"t":14.58,"w":"another"},{"t":14.58,"w":"925"},{"t":14.58,"w":"English"},{"t":14.58,"w":"lesson."},{"t":20.07,"w":"Today"},{"t":20.07,"w":"we’re"},{"t":20.07,"w":"going"},{"t":20.07,"w":"to"},{"t":20.07,"w":"learn"},{"t":20.07,"w":"how"},{"t":20.07,"w":"to"},{"t":20.07,"w":"explain"},{"t":20.07,"w":"a"},{"t":20.07,"w":"problem"},{"t":20.07,"w":"in"},{"t":20.07,"w":"English."},{"t":28.15,"w":"Much"},{"t":28.15,"w":"of"},{"t":28.15,"w":"your"},{"t":28.15,"w":"work"},{"t":28.15,"w":"probably"},{"t":28.15,"w":"involves"},{"t":28.15,"w":"solving"},{"t":28.15,"w":"problems."},{"t":33.61,"w":"And"},{"t":33.61,"w":"sometimes"},{"t":33.61,"w":"we"},{"t":33.61,"w":"can’t"},{"t":33.61,"w":"do"},{"t":33.61,"w":"it"},{"t":33.61,"w":"alone."},{"t":38.129,"w":"But"},{"t":38.129,"w":"cooperating"},{"t":38.129,"w":"with"},{"t":38.129,"w":"someone"},{"t":38.129,"w":"to"},{"t":38.129,"w":"solve"},{"t":38.129,"w":"a"},{"t":38.129,"w":"problem"},{"t":38.129,"w":"means"},{"t":38.129,"w":"explaining"},{"t":38.129,"w":"it"},{"t":38.129,"w":"clearly."},{"t":46.08,"w":"Before"},{"t":46.08,"w":"you"},{"t":46.08,"w":"explain"},{"t":46.08,"w":"the"},{"t":46.08,"w":"problem"},{"t":46.08,"w":"however,"},{"t":46.08,"w":"you’ll"},{"t":46.08,"w":"need"},{"t":46.08,"w":"to"},{"t":46.08,"w":"ask"},{"t":46.08,"w":"for"},{"t":46.08,"w":"help."},{"t":52.719,"w":"Two"},{"t":52.719,"w":"really"},{"t":52.719,"w":"useful"},{"t":52.719,"w":"words"},{"t":52.719,"w":"in"},{"t":52.719,"w":"this"},{"t":52.719,"w":"situation"},{"t":52.719,"w":"are"},{"t":52.719,"w":"“would”"},{"t":52.719,"w":"and"},{"t":52.719,"w":"“could.”"},{"t":62.19,"w":"These"},{"t":62.19,"w":"words"},{"t":6